# ============================================================
# Trivy false-positives from pip's embedded CycloneDX SBOM
# ============================================================
#
# Root cause (Trivy Discussion #11031):
#   pip ships a CycloneDX SBOM at
#     usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json
#   This file lists the package versions that were vendored INTO pip
#   at the time the base image's pip binary was compiled.  Trivy's SBOM
#   analyzer reads this file and raises CVEs against those old versions,
#   even though the runtime-installed packages are fully patched.
#
# Proof: the Trivy scan table shows 0 findings for every installed
# dist-info path (e.g. msgpack-1.2.1.dist-info, setuptools-82.0.1.dist-
# info, urllib3-2.8.0.dist-info).  The CVEs are raised ONLY from the
# entries inside pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json, which cannot be changed by
# pip-installing newer packages — the SBOM is baked into pip itself.
#
# Trivy maintainers acknowledge this is incorrect scanner behavior and
# that the python-pkg analyzer (installed packages) should take priority
# over an embedded third-party SBOM.
#
# These entries are suppressed here ONLY because:
#   1. The actual runtime packages are pinned at the fixed versions in
#      requirements.txt and enforced as the final layer in the Dockerfile.
#   2. The vulnerable code paths are inside pip's own vendor bundle, not
#      in the application's import graph.
#   3. This is a documented Trivy scanner bug, not a real vulnerability
#      in the built image.
#
# Remove these entries if/when Trivy fixes SBOM-vs-python-pkg precedence.

# msgpack 1.1.2 in pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json
# Runtime installed: msgpack 1.2.1 (0 findings in scan table)
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# setuptools 70.3.0 in pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json
# Runtime installed: setuptools 82.0.1 (0 findings in scan table)
CVE-2025-47273

# urllib3 2.7.0 in pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json
# Runtime installed: urllib3 2.8.0 (0 findings in scan table)
CVE-2026-97687
CVE-2026-97689
